  "preamble": "Lowering of a high front vowel to open-mid [ɛ] is best attested in the Slavic vocalization of the front yer. Proto-Slavic *ь, a short or reduced high front vowel ([ĭ], [ɪ]) continuing Proto-Indo-European short *i, was lost in weak position but became a full vowel in strong position (Havlík's law). In Polish, Czech and Bulgarian, whose mid front vowel is open-mid [ɛ], the result is [ɛ]: Proto-Slavic *dьnь 'day' > Polish dzień [ˈd͡ʑɛɲ], Czech den [ˈdɛn], Bulgarian ден [dɛn]. Russian and Slovak have the same change but a closer [e].",
